<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    WHY LIBRARY VALIDATION IS DISABLED. Hive's semantic-memory leg loads
    third-party native modules that cannot live inside the single-file binary:
    fastembed's @anush008/tokenizers napi binding and onnxruntime-node's
    dylibs, unpacked into ~/.hive/tools/embeddings. They come from npm
    adhoc/linker-signed with TeamIdentifier=not set, and a hardened runtime
    refuses to dlopen a library whose Team ID differs from the process's:
    "mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs".
    Without this entitlement a Developer-ID-signed hive cannot load them at
    all, which is why semantic memory never worked in a signed release.

    WHAT IT PERMITS: the hive process may load libraries regardless of who
    signed them — not only these. Their bytes are still anchored by the
    SHA-256 pinned in the Ed25519-signed release manifest, verified before
    anything is unpacked, but nothing re-checks that digest at load time.
    Note this is not the only unsigned code the process runs: the runtime's
    bundle (dist/entry.js) is plain JavaScript loaded from the same directory
    with no signature check, so library validation was not providing code
    integrity for this process before the entitlement either.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
